**Today's thread — "The Price Stopped Asking" (The Beam, Pillars 4 / 9 / 5).** The conventional mortgage credit-risk premium has collapsed to 31.6bps on a 21-day average, the 7.5th percentile since 2017 and roughly half the 59.5bps that prevailed pre-2020. Widen to the full cross-section and the ordering inverts: FHA at 6.435% and VA at 6.289% both price *below* the 6.643% charged to a 740-plus borrower with twenty percent down. FHA crossed under prime conventional in 2023 and has stayed there three years running. Meanwhile delinquency has climbed from a 1.70% trough to 1.89%, single-family starts fell to 895k, and builder sentiment sits at 34. The price of mortgage credit has stopped carrying borrower information, so the sorting moved from price to access.

Ten charts rendered to brand standard, five for the piece and five for the paid supplementary pack. Six sharpened interview questions with the data points mapped under each. Three headlines.

Two things worth knowing, both logged in `build_notes.md`. I killed a Two Economies cohort-homeownership angle because the data ran the opposite way (Black-alone homeownership is *up* 1.5pts year over year), and I dropped a strong JOLTS labor-flows thread because yesterday's Beam already pointed Pillar 1 at Friday's payrolls. Two chart annotations failed verification against the series and were corrected before the final render.

The DB was healthy this run, unlike Aug 3. Nothing was published anywhere.

